<style>
  body {
    margin: 0;
    padding: 0;
    overflow: hidden;
    background: #fafafa;
    font-family: 'Helvetica Neue', Helvetica, Arial, sans-serif;
  }

  #coherence-container {
    position: fixed;
    inset: 0;
  }

  /* ── modal overlay ── */
  #plct-modal {
    position: fixed;
    inset: 0;
    z-index: 100;
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    justify-content: center;
    background: rgba(250, 250, 250, 0.88);
    backdrop-filter: blur(3px);
  }

  #plct-modal.hidden {
    display: none;
  }

  #plct-modal-inner {
    background: #fff;
    border: 1px solid #ddd;
    max-width: 680px;
    width: calc(100% - 3rem);
    max-height: 82vh;
    overflow-y: auto;
    padding: 3rem 3.5rem;
    position: relative;
  }

  #plct-modal-inner h1 {
    font-size: 0.82rem;
    font-weight: 500;
    letter-spacing: 0.12em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: #111;
    margin: 0 0 0.25rem;
  }

  #plct-modal-inner .byline {
    font-size: 0.68rem;
    color: #999;
    letter-spacing: 0.06em;
    margin-bottom: 2rem;
  }

  #plct-modal-inner h2 {
    font-size: 0.72rem;
    font-weight: 500;
    letter-spacing: 0.1em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: #333;
    margin: 1.8rem 0 0.5rem;
    border-bottom: 1px solid #eee;
    padding-bottom: 0.3rem;
  }

  #plct-modal-inner p {
    font-size: 0.8rem;
    line-height: 1.7;
    color: #444;
    margin: 0 0 0.8rem;
  }

  #plct-modal-inner .tier-table {
    width: 100%;
    border-collapse: collapse;
    font-size: 0.73rem;
    margin: 0.5rem 0 1rem;
  }

  #plct-modal-inner .tier-table th {
    text-align: left;
    font-weight: 500;
    letter-spacing: 0.06em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    font-size: 0.62rem;
    color: #999;
    border-bottom: 1px solid #eee;
    padding: 0.3rem 0.5rem 0.3rem 0;
  }

  #plct-modal-inner .tier-table td {
    padding: 0.3rem 0.5rem 0.3rem 0;
    color: #333;
    border-bottom: 1px solid #f3f3f3;
    vertical-align: top;
  }

  #plct-modal-inner .tier-table td:first-child {
    font-weight: 500;
    color: #111;
    white-space: nowrap;
  }

  #plct-modal-inner .monospace {
    font-family: 'Courier New', monospace;
    font-size: 0.72rem;
    color: #555;
    background: #f8f8f8;
    padding: 0.15rem 0.35rem;
    border-radius: 2px;
  }

  #plct-close {
    position: absolute;
    top: 1.2rem;
    right: 1.4rem;
    background: none;
    border: none;
    font-size: 1.1rem;
    color: #aaa;
    cursor: pointer;
    line-height: 1;
    padding: 0.2rem 0.4rem;
  }

  #plct-close:hover {
    color: #333;
  }

  #plct-open {
    position: fixed;
    bottom: 1.8rem;
    right: 2rem;
    z-index: 50;
    background: none;
    border: 1px solid #ccc;
    font-family: 'Helvetica Neue', Helvetica, Arial, sans-serif;
    font-size: 0.62rem;
    letter-spacing: 0.12em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: #888;
    padding: 0.45rem 0.9rem;
    cursor: pointer;
  }

  #plct-open:hover {
    color: #111;
    border-color: #888;
  }

  /* ── scene label ── */
  #scene-label {
    position: fixed;
    top: 1.8rem;
    left: 2rem;
    font-size: 0.65rem;
    letter-spacing: 0.16em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: #bbb;
    z-index: 10;
    pointer-events: none;
  }
</style>

<div id="coherence-container"></div>

<div id="scene-label">Coherence — PLCT / Russell</div>

<!-- Modal -->
<div id="plct-modal">
  <div id="plct-modal-inner">
    <button id="plct-close" aria-label="Close">&times;</button>

    <h1>Prime Lattice Coherence Framework</h1>
    <div class="byline">
      Griff Gurwell &mdash; CTF Framework Research Series &mdash; June 2026
    </div>

    <p>
      The PLCT classifies every positive integer by its prime factor set and assigns it to one of four tiers.
      Only mechanisms built from primes &#x7B;2, 3&#x7D; maintain phase coherence at all recursive scales.
      Walter Russell intuited this structure empirically in 1926.
      The PLCT provides the derivation he was reaching for.
    </p>

    <h2>Tiers</h2>
    <table class="tier-table">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Tier</th>
          <th>Primes</th>
          <th>Character</th>
          <th>Examples</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td>T1</td>
          <td>&#x7B;2, 3&#x7D;</td>
          <td>Maximally coherent — zero drift at all scales</td>
          <td><span class="monospace">2, 3, 4, 6, 8, 12, 24, 144</span></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>T2</td>
          <td>&#x7B;2, 3, 5&#x7D;</td>
          <td>Coherent through the temporal gateway prime 5; slow drift</td>
          <td><span class="monospace">5, 10, 30, 60, 360</span></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>T3</td>
          <td>&#x7B;2, 3, 5, 7&#x7D;</td>
          <td>Hard Wall boundary — first prime exiting coherent zone</td>
          <td><span class="monospace">7, 14, 21, 42</span></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>T4</td>
          <td>≥ 11</td>
          <td>Incoherent — unbounded logarithmic drift at all scales</td>
          <td><span class="monospace">11, 13, 22, 86, 118</span></td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <h2>Three Zones (mod 9)</h2>
    <table class="tier-table">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Zone</th>
          <th>Residues</th>
          <th>Character</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td>Spine</td>
          <td><span class="monospace">0, 3, 6 mod 9</span></td>
          <td>Static ground state. No prime > 3. Mathematical vacuum.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Hard Wall</td>
          <td><span class="monospace">1, 4, 7 mod 9</span></td>
          <td>Exclusion boundary. Prime pairs forbidden.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Temporal</td>
          <td><span class="monospace">2, 5, 8 mod 9</span></td>
          <td>Kinetically active. Primes 2× denser than Hard Wall.</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <h2>Russell's Ring Multipliers — All T1</h2>
    <p>
      Russell's Crystallization Chart No. 1 uses ring multipliers
      <span class="monospace">&#x7B;3, 4, 8, 12, 24&#x7D;</span>.
      Every one is a pure Tier-1 number built from primes &#x7B;2, 3&#x7D; only.
      Russell found them by observation. The PLCT's Lock-Out Theorem proves why they are the only multipliers
      that operate coherently at all scales.
    </p>

    <h2>Noble Gas Stability Predicted by Tier</h2>
    <table class="tier-table">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Gas</th>
          <th>Z</th>
          <th>Tier</th>
          <th>Zone</th>
          <th>Stability</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>He</td><td>2</td><td>T1</td><td>Temporal</td><td>Stable, inert</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Ne</td><td>10</td><td>T2</td><td>Hard Wall</td><td>Stable, inert</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Ar</td><td>18</td><td>T1</td><td>Spine</td><td>Stable, inert</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Kr</td><td>36</td><td>T1</td><td>Spine</td><td>Stable, inert</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Xe</td><td>54</td><td>T1</td><td>Spine</td><td>Stable, inert</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Rn</td><td>86</td><td>T4</td><td>Temporal</td><td>Radioactive (3.8-day half-life)</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Og</td><td>118</td><td>T4</td><td>Hard Wall</td><td>Extremely unstable (ms half-life)</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <h2>Crystallographic Lock-Out Theorem</h2>
    <p>
      The permitted crystal rotation orders <span class="monospace">&#x7B;1, 2, 3, 4, 6&#x7D;</span> are exactly
      the Tier-1 numbers in that range.
      The first two <em>forbidden</em> orders are the temporal gateway prime
      <span class="monospace">5</span> (T2) and the Hard Wall prime
      <span class="monospace">7</span> (T3).
      The crystallographic restriction theorem is the Lock-Out Theorem applied to spatial tiling.
    </p>

    <h2>Testable Prediction: Diamond vs Silicon</h2>
    <p>
      Carbon (Z=6, T1/Spine) and Silicon (Z=14, T3/Hard Wall) share the same cubic diamond crystal structure.
      The PLCT predicts T1 elements achieve higher crystal quality even with identical structure.
      Diamond outperforms silicon by 1.5× in hardness, 2.5× in melting point, and 14.7× in thermal conductivity —
      exactly as tier predicts.
    </p>

    <h2>The Acoustic Isomorphism</h2>
    <p>
      The Western musical system and the &#x7B;2&#x1D43; × 3&#x1D47;&#x7D; prime lattice are mathematically identical.
      T1 primes &#x7B;2, 3&#x7D; generate octaves and perfect fifths.
      The temporal gateway prime 5 generates the major third.
      The Hard Wall prime 7 generates the harmonic seventh — the first interval that cannot be integrated without drift.
      Russell's "crystals woven by musical tones" is not metaphor. It is the same integer lattice projected into two domains.
    </p>
  </div>
</div>

<button id="plct-open">PLCT notes</button>

<script>
  window.addEventListener("DOMContentLoaded", () => {
    window.coherence();

    const modal = document.getElementById('plct-modal');
    document.getElementById('plct-close').addEventListener('click', () => {
      modal.classList.add('hidden');
    });
    document.getElementById('plct-open').addEventListener('click', () => {
      modal.classList.remove('hidden');
    });
    document.addEventListener('keydown', e => {
      if (e.key === 'Escape') modal.classList.add('hidden');
    });
  });
</script>
